\title{Functorial Information Loss:\\Entropy, Fibers, and Finite Object Maps}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A functor between finite categories induces a deterministic map on objects, and such a map can be studied as an information channel fed by the uniform distribution on its source. Two quantities must be distinguished: the Shannon entropy of the output object and the expected logarithmic cardinality of the observed fiber. We prove that the fiber probabilities form a distribution and establish the exact chain rule
\[
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)+L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=\log |A|.
\]
Fiber loss vanishes exactly when the object map is injective. For uniform nonempty fibers of size $k$ and $m$ attained outputs, the loss is $\log k=\log(|A|/m)$, whereas output entropy is $\log m$. Thus the logarithmic quotient is an information-loss term, not output entropy. We discuss constant, injective, uniform, and nonuniform examples; efficient algorithms; categorical scope; and extensions involving composition, groupoid cardinality, morphisms, and measure.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Functors are often described as forgetting structure. A forgetful construction discards decoration; an invariant sends many objects to one classification; a quotient merges distinctions. For finite collections, this language admits a precise information-theoretic interpretation. Let $A$ be a nonempty finite source and let $f:A\to B$ be the map on objects. Choose a source uniformly and observe only its image. The result is a deterministic channel whose fibers record the source objects that have become indistinguishable.

There are two distinct statistics. Shannon entropy measures uncertainty in the visible output. It does not itself measure erasure: a constant map has output entropy zero while identifying every source. The information lost is instead the conditional uncertainty of the source after the output is known. Under a uniform source, this is the expected logarithm of the observed fiber size.

We therefore separate output entropy from fiber loss and prove that their sum is the entropy $\log|A|$ of the original uniform source. This repairs the interpretation of the familiar logarithmic quotient formula and establishes the finite foundation for broader categorical constructions. All logarithms are natural, so information is measured in nats.

\section{Finite deterministic object channels}

Let $A$ and $B$ be finite sets, assume $A\ne\varnothing$, and fix $f:A\to B$.

\begin{definition}[Fiber]
For $b\in B$, the fiber over $b$ is
\[
A_b=f^{-1}(b)=\{a\in A:f(a)=b\}.
\]
The output $b$ is \emph{attained} when $A_b\ne\varnothing$.
\end{definition}

The fibers partition $A$, and hence
\[
|A|=\sum_{b\in B}|A_b|.
\]

\begin{definition}[Output probability]
When a source is chosen uniformly, define
\[
p_b=\frac{|A_b|}{|A|}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Normalization]
The family $(p_b)_{b\in B}$ is a probability distribution: $p_b\ge0$ and
\[
\sum_{b\in B}p_b=1.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity follows from cardinalities. The fiber partition gives
\[
\sum_{b\in B}p_b=\frac{1}{|A|}\sum_{b\in B}|A_b|=1.
\]
The denominator is positive because $A$ is nonempty.
\end{proof}

We use the entropy convention $0\log0=0$.

\begin{definition}[Output object entropy]
The entropy of the observed output is
\[
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)=-\sum_{b\in B}p_b\log p_b.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Expected fiber loss]
The information loss of the object map is
\[
L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=\sum_{b\in B}p_b\log|A_b|,
\]
where an empty fiber contributes zero.
\end{definition}

Conditioned on an attained output $b$, the posterior on $A_b$ is uniform, because every source had the same prior probability. Its entropy is $\log|A_b|$. Thus $L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)$ is exactly the expected conditional source entropy given the output. Both statistics are nonnegative.

\section{The entropy--loss chain rule}

\begin{theorem}[Entropy--Loss Chain Rule]
For every map $f:A\to B$ from a nonempty finite set to a finite set,
\[
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)+L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=\log|A|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For every attained $b$,
\[
p_b=\frac{|A_b|}{|A|},
\qquad
-\log p_b+\log|A_b|=\log|A|.
\]
Multiply by $p_b$ and sum. Empty fibers contribute zero, and normalization yields
\begin{align*}
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)+L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)
&=\sum_{b\in B}p_b\bigl(-\log p_b+\log|A_b|\bigr)\\
&=\sum_{b\in B}p_b\log|A|=\log|A|.
\end{align*}
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Complementarity]
One has
\[
L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=\log|A|-H_{\mathrm{out}}(f).
\]
For fixed source size, maximizing output entropy is equivalent to minimizing fiber loss.
\end{corollary}

The identity is an exact information ledger: output entropy records visible diversity, while fiber loss records hidden source ambiguity.

\section{Zero loss and object injectivity}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-Loss Characterization]
For $A$ nonempty and finite,
\[
L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
f\text{ is injective}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $f$ is injective, every attained fiber has size one, so every summand contains $\log1=0$. Conversely, if $f$ is not injective, some fiber contains at least two elements. It has positive probability and strictly positive logarithmic cardinality. Its contribution is positive, while all other contributions are nonnegative. Hence the total loss is positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $f$ is injective, then $H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)=\log|A|$.
\end{corollary}

For a functor $F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D$, faithfulness means that each function
\[
\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C}(X,Y)\longrightarrow
\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal D}(F(X),F(Y))
\]
is injective. That morphism condition is distinct from injectivity of $X\mapsto F(X)$. Consequently, zero fiber loss characterizes object injectivity, not categorical faithfulness. Detecting faithfulness requires a morphism-level loss component.

\section{Uniform fibers}

\begin{definition}[Uniform-fiber map]
A map $f:A\to B$ has uniform nonempty fibers of size $k$ when $|A_b|=k$ for every attained $b$. Write $m=|f(A)|$ for the number of attained outputs. Then $|A|=mk$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform-Fiber Loss Formula]
If all nonempty fibers have size $k$, then
\[
L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=\log k
=\log\frac{|A|}{m}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every attained output has probability $k/|A|=1/m$. The logarithmic fiber size is constantly $\log k$ on the support, so its expectation is $\log k$. The quotient form follows from $|A|=mk$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform-Fiber Output Formula]
Under the same hypotheses,
\[
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)=\log m.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The output is uniform on $m$ values, each of probability $1/m$. Therefore
\[
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)
=-m\frac1m\log\frac1m=\log m.
\]
\end{proof}

Thus $\log(|A|/m)=\log k$ is loss, whereas $\log m$ is output entropy. Their sum is $\log|A|$.

\section{Examples and extremal cases}

\begin{proposition}[Constant map]
If $f:A\to B$ is constant, then
\[
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)=0,
\qquad
L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=\log|A|.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
One output occurs with probability one, and its fiber is all of $A$.
\end{proof}

Together with nonnegativity and the chain rule, this shows
\[
0\le L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)\le\log|A|.
\]
Constant maps attain the upper endpoint; injective maps attain the lower endpoint.

\begin{example}[Six-to-three residue channel]
Let $A=\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$, $B=\{0,1,2\}$, and send each integer to its residue modulo $3$. The fibers are
\[
\{0,3\},\qquad\{1,4\},\qquad\{2,5\}.
\]
Thus $k=2$ and $m=3$, giving
\[
L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=\log2,
\qquad
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)=\log3,
\]
and $\log2+\log3=\log6$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Nonuniform fibers]
For fiber sizes $1$, $2$, and $3$, the source size is $6$ and
\[
H_{\mathrm{out}}
=-\frac16\log\frac16-\frac26\log\frac26-\frac36\log\frac36,
\]
while
\[
L_{\mathrm{fib}}=\frac16\log1+\frac26\log2+\frac36\log3.
\]
Neither statistic is generally the logarithm of one integer, but their sum is exactly $\log6$. This is why expected log fiber size, rather than log average fiber size, is the natural loss for nonuniform maps.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

Suppose a finite map is represented by a list of output labels, one for each source.

\paragraph{Fiber-entropy decomposition.}
Count the frequency $c_b$ of each attained label in one pass. With $n=|A|$, set $p_b=c_b/n$, accumulate $-p_b\log p_b$ and $p_b\log c_b$, and compare their sum with $\log n$. Using a hash table, the expected time is $O(n)$ and memory is $O(m)$ for $m$ attained labels. A sorting implementation uses $O(n\log n)$ time.

\paragraph{Uniform-fiber certification.}
After constructing the frequency table, check whether all positive counts equal one value $k$. If so, let $m$ be the number of labels and check $n=mk$. The closed formulas are then $H_{\mathrm{out}}=\log m$ and $L_{\mathrm{fib}}=\log k$. Expected time is $O(n)$ and memory $O(m)$.

\paragraph{Zero-loss diagnostic.}
Store the first source seen for each output. A later distinct source with the same output is a collision witness and proves positive loss. If no collision occurs, the map is injective and has zero loss. This takes expected $O(n)$ time, $O(m)$ memory, and may terminate early.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Classification by invariants.}
On a finite sampled family, mapping an object to dimension, rank, or another invariant gives a deterministic channel. Output entropy measures diversity among invariant values; fiber loss measures unresolved ambiguity among objects sharing a value.

\paragraph{Clustering and coarse-graining.}
A cluster label is an output. Label entropy measures balance across clusters, while expected log cluster size measures ambiguity about the original data point. Equal-sized clusters give the uniform formulas, and the chain rule remains exact without equality.

\paragraph{Privacy.}
An anonymization rule sends records to released equivalence classes. Fiber loss captures the combinatorial ambiguity of recovering a uniformly sampled record. It is not a complete privacy metric, since nonuniform priors, side information, and semantic sensitivity alter the posterior.

\paragraph{Computation.}
A many-to-one operation erases distinctions. If a surrounding computation must remain reversible, enough auxiliary information must be retained to distinguish states inside each fiber. A uniform $k$-to-one operation hides $\log k$ nats per uniformly sampled input.

\section{Scope and limitations}

The formulas assume a uniform source. For a general prior $q_a$, the output probability is $p_b=\sum_{a\in A_b}q_a$, and the posterior within a fiber need not be uniform. The appropriate loss is then conditional entropy, not expected log cardinality.

Raw object counts are also not invariant under equivalence of categories: equivalent categories may display different numbers of isomorphic copies. A skeleton removes such copies, while finite groupoids suggest a finer correction using reciprocal automorphism weights.

For infinite collections, a uniform probability distribution usually does not exist. One cannot define probabilities by dividing fiber sizes by a total number of objects in categories of all sets, groups, or topological spaces. Moreover, one infinite fiber does not force infinite expected loss; divergence depends on its probability weight. Infinite extensions therefore require measures and measurable conditional entropy.

Finally, object loss cannot detect identified morphisms. A complete categorical theory should combine distributions on objects with conditional distributions on hom-sets and split total loss into object and morphism components.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Composable finite functors.}
For composable functors between finite skeletal categories, a relative chain rule should weight conditional loss by the actual intermediate pushforward distribution. Conditional-uniformity hypotheses may characterize when simpler stagewise logarithmic formulas hold.

\paragraph{Groupoid-cardinality entropy.}
For finite groupoids, an isomorphism class represented by $x$ should receive weight proportional to $1/|\operatorname{Aut}(x)|$. Essentially surjective functors with homotopically uniform fibers may satisfy a chain rule using homotopy fiber cardinality, producing an equivalence-invariant theory.

\paragraph{Objects and morphisms.}
For finite categories with distributions on objects and conditional distributions on hom-sets, one may seek separate object and morphism losses. Vanishing total loss should express injectivity on supported objects together with faithfulness on supported hom-sets.

\paragraph{Measured infinite categories.}
For essentially small categories whose isomorphism classes form countable or standard Borel spaces, probability measures can replace uniform counting. Infinite loss should mean divergence of measurable conditional entropy, not merely the presence of an infinite fiber.

\section{Conclusion}

A finite object map divides the entropy $\log|A|$ of a uniform source into visible output entropy and hidden fiber ambiguity:
\[
H_{\mathrm{out}}(f)+L_{\mathrm{fib}}(f)=\log|A|.
\]
Zero loss is equivalent to object injectivity. Uniform fibers of size $k$ give loss $\log k$, while $m$ attained outputs give entropy $\log m$. This theory neither confuses object injectivity with faithfulness nor extends unweighted counting to infinite categories. Instead, it supplies a precise foundation on which compositional, equivalence-invariant, morphism-sensitive, and measurable notions of categorical information can be built.

\end{document}

