If the map is injective, every attained fiber has size one and every logarithmic term is zero. If it is not injective, some positive-probability fiber has size at least two; that term is positive and all remaining terms are nonnegative. Thus loss vanishes exactly for object-injective maps. This is distinct from [faithful functors](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_and_faithful_functors), which concern morphisms.
</details>

## 4. Uniform maps and the quotient formula

For $m$ attained outputs and common fiber size $k$, the source size is $mk$. The retained entropy is $\log m$ and the loss is $\log k=\log(|A|/m)$.

